In Spring Boot, I’d implement caching using Spring Cache abstraction. The idea is: instead of querying the database every time,
the first request loads the data and stores it in a cache; subsequent requests get it from the cache.

1. Add dependency

For example, with Redis:

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-cache</artifactId>
</dependency>

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-redis</artifactId>
</dependency>



2. Enable caching
@SpringBootApplication
@EnableCaching
public class Application {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args);
    }
}

@EnableCaching tells Spring to create the caching infrastructure and intercept methods annotated with cache annotations.

3. Use @Cacheable

Suppose we have:

@Service
public class UserService {

    @Cacheable(value = "users", key = "#id")
    public User getUser(Long id) {

        System.out.println("Calling database...");

        return userRepository.findById(id)
                .orElseThrow();
    }
}

Now:

GET /users/10
First request
Controller
   ↓
Service
   ↓
Cache → MISS
   ↓
Database
   ↓
User returned
   ↓
User stored in cache
Second request
Controller
   ↓
Service
   ↓
Cache → HIT
   ↓
User returned

The database isn't called on the second request.

4. @CachePut

Use this when you want to execute the method and update the cache.

@CachePut(value = "users", key = "#user.id")
public User updateUser(User user) {

    return userRepository.save(user);
}

Unlike @Cacheable, @CachePut does not skip the method execution.

So:

@Cacheable → cache hit → method doesn't execute
@CachePut  → method always executes → cache gets updated
5. @CacheEvict

When data is deleted or becomes invalid:

@CacheEvict(value = "users", key = "#id")
public void deleteUser(Long id) {

    userRepository.deleteById(id);
}

This removes:

users::10

from the cache.

You can also clear the entire cache:

@CacheEvict(value = "users", allEntries = true)
public void clearUsersCache() {
}
6. @Caching

Sometimes you need multiple cache operations:

@Caching(
    put = {
        @CachePut(value = "users", key = "#user.id")
    },
    evict = {
        @CacheEvict(value = "userList", allEntries = true)
    }
)
public User updateUser(User user) {

    return userRepository.save(user);
}

Here we:

Update the individual user's cache.
Invalidate the cached user list.
7. Redis configuration

For Redis, you can configure:

spring:
  data:
    redis:
      host: localhost
      port: 6379

  cache:
    type: redis
    redis:
      time-to-live: 600000

Here the cache entries have a TTL of 10 minutes.

For example:

users::10
    ↓
User object
    ↓
TTL = 10 minutes

After 10 minutes, Redis removes/invalidates the entry and the next request goes to the database again.

8. Important interview point: Spring Cache uses proxies

This is similar to the Spring AOP question you asked earlier.

When you write:

@Cacheable("users")
public User getUser(Long id) {
    ...
}

Spring doesn't modify your method directly.

Conceptually:

Client
  ↓
Spring Proxy
  ↓
Check cache
  ↓
 ┌───────────────┐
 │ Cache HIT?    │
 └───────────────┘
    ↓        ↓
   YES       NO
    ↓        ↓
 return   execute method
             ↓
          Database
             ↓
        put in cache

That's why self-invocation can bypass caching:

public void methodA() {
    methodB();   // @Cacheable on methodB may be bypassed
}

@Cacheable("users")
public User methodB() {
    ...
}

Because the call doesn't go through the Spring proxy.

9. What would I choose in a real backend?

For a simple single-instance application:

Spring Cache
     ↓
Caffeine
     ↓
In-memory cache

For a distributed microservices application:

Spring Cache
     ↓
Redis
     ↓
Shared distributed cache

For example:

             ┌── Service Instance 1 ──┐
Client ──────┤                         ├── Redis
             ├── Service Instance 2 ──┤
             └── Service Instance 3 ──┘

All instances can access the same cached data.

Interview answer

"I would use Spring Cache abstraction with @EnableCaching. For read-heavy operations, I'd use @Cacheable to check the cache before hitting the database.
For updates, I'd use @CachePut or evict the stale entry with @CacheEvict. In a distributed Spring Boot application,
I'd generally use Redis as the cache provider and configure TTL to prevent stale data from remaining indefinitely.
I'd also pay attention to cache keys, serialization, eviction strategy, and cache consistency."